---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import PageHead from '../../components/PageHead.astro';
import '../../styles/v9.css';

// Every figure on this page is copied from the Phase 1 report and §1–10 of the mtime report.
const arms = [
	{
		arm: 'Uninformed',
		set: 'Pre-registered 30',
		pairs: '30',
		orig: '21',
		norm: '20',
		disc: '4 : 3',
		p: '1.0',
		diff: '+3.3 pp (−13.3 to +20.0)',
	},
	{
		arm: '',
		set: 'All pairs',
		pairs: '73',
		orig: '50',
		norm: '47',
		disc: '9 : 6',
		p: '0.61',
		diff: '+4.1 pp (−5.5 to +15.1)',
	},
	{
		arm: 'Informed (hint)',
		set: 'Pre-registered 30',
		pairs: '30',
		orig: '20',
		norm: '17',
		disc: '7 : 4',
		p: '0.55',
		diff: '+10.0 pp (−10.0 to +30.0)',
	},
	{
		arm: '',
		set: 'All pairs',
		pairs: '73',
		orig: '50',
		norm: '45',
		disc: '14 : 9',
		p: '0.40',
		diff: '+6.85 pp (−5.5 to +19.2)',
	},
	{
		arm: 'Scaffold (forced list)',
		set: 'Pre-registered 30',
		pairs: '30',
		orig: '22',
		norm: '13',
		disc: '12 : 3',
		p: '0.035',
		diff: '+30.0 pp (+6.7 to +53.3)',
	},
	{
		arm: '',
		set: 'All pairs (decision rule)',
		pairs: '73',
		orig: '51',
		norm: '42',
		disc: '18 : 9',
		p: '0.12',
		diff: '+12.3 pp (−1.4 to +26.0)',
	},
];

const rankers = [
	{ ranker: 'Modification time, newest first', hit1: '0.92 (0.88–0.96)', hit5: '1.00 (1.00–1.00)', p3: '0.79 (0.74–0.84)' },
	{ ranker: 'Issue text vs file paths (BM25)', hit1: '0.23 (0.15–0.33)', hit5: '0.56 (0.45–0.67)', p3: '0.22 (0.16–0.28)' },
	{ ranker: 'File size, largest first', hit1: '0.08 (0.03–0.15)', hit5: '0.27 (0.17–0.37)', p3: '0.08 (0.03–0.13)' },
	{ ranker: 'Random', hit1: '0.03 (0.02–0.04)', hit5: '0.11 (0.07–0.16)', p3: '0.03 (0.02–0.04)' },
];
---

<Layout
	title="DRAFT — MiMo RL environments audit · Cupel"
	description="Draft, not for publication."
	analytics={false}
	noindex={true}
>
	<SiteHeader current="cupel" />
		<PageHead back={{ href: '/cupel', label: 'Cupel' }} kicker="Cupel · Audit · Draft" animate={true}>
			MiMo RL environments (Xiaomi)
		</PageHead>

		<main class="v9">
			<div class="pad">
				<div class="grid12">
					<div class="paper">
						<div class="callout" role="note">
							<span class="lbl">// draft</span>
							<p>
								<strong>DRAFT, not for publication.</strong> Final numbers, including the sham-list control in § 07.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="paper-hero">
						<div class="head">
							<p class="sub">
								Xiaomi's fix for the git-history leak holds. A second leak, through file modification
								times, is still present in most tasks. <em>
									It reveals which files to change, not how to change them, and we could not show that
									it helps an agent solve more tasks.
								</em>
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="meta-rail">
							<div class="row"><span>// suite</span><span>MiMo-V2.6-RL-oss</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// split</span><span>code · 2,602 tasks</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// sample</span><span>300 tasks</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// severity</span><span>Low</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// status</span><span>draft</span></div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="paper">
						<div class="stat-strip">
							<div class="stat">
								<span class="idx">// git-history fix present</span>
								<span class="val">0 / 300</span>
							</div>
							<div class="stat">
								<span class="idx">// mtime side channel</span>
								<span class="val accent">85.3%</span>
							</div>
							<div class="stat">
								<span class="idx">// severity</span>
								<span class="val">Low</span>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 01</span><span class="ttl">// what we tested</span><span class="meta">scope</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="what we tested">
						<h2>Scope</h2>
						<p>
							The coding split of Xiaomi's open RL environments, <code class="code-inline"
								>XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-V2.6-RL-oss</code
							> at revision <code class="code-inline">971cab2ab421fd88db29770db0d0e52014b63615</code>
							(2026-10-18), with one container image per task. The split has 2,602 tasks. On 2026-10-18,
							96 tasks were dropped (2,698 → 2,602) with no change to the tasks that were kept, and all
							2,602 image tags were last pushed on 2026-10-16.
						</p>
						<p>
							We inspected a stratified random sample of <strong>300 tasks</strong>, stratified by language
							and image size, statically: the file system and git object store of each image, without
							running the task. All 300 image digests were recorded. None changed during the scan.
						</p>
						<p>
							We checked the git object store for anything recoverable beyond the task's base commit, file
							modification times, stale bytecode and other build artefacts, backup and patch residue,
							hidden test files on disk, and credential files. To check that these checks find what they
							look for, we planted leaks of <strong>23 types</strong> into clean task images, 5 images per
							type. All <strong>115 / 115</strong> were detected, with no detections on the unplanted
							images. The plants were written by the same author as the checks, so this shows the checks
							do what they are specified to do, not that they cover unknown techniques.
						</p>
						<p>
							Limits. The grading script is injected at grading time and is not in any of the 300 images, so
							the grader cannot be audited statically. Network egress is a property of the runtime harness
							and was not judged either way. No gold patch ships with the dataset, so solvability and the
							pass-rate band were not checked.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 02</span><span class="ttl">// the git-history fix holds</span><span
							class="meta">0 / 300</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="the git-history fix holds">
						<h2>No recoverable fix in git</h2>
						<p>
							<a href="https://www.vals.ai/blogs/mimo-reward-hacking">Vals AI's report</a> (7 Oct 2026) found
							the reference fix surviving as unreachable git objects in 1,795 of 2,698 tasks (66.5%), on the
							images that existed before they were re-pushed. We looked for the same class on the current
							images.
						</p>
						<p>
							In <strong>0 of 300</strong> tasks did we find a recoverable commit later than the task's base
							(95% Wilson CI 0–1.26%). There were no unreachable objects, no commits outside the base
							history, and no reflogs, stash, pseudo-refs or remote refs in any primary repository. The
							result was 0 in every one of the 12 strata.
						</p>
						<p>
							The git packs inside the images are dated 2026-10-09 to 2026-10-13. That is consistent with
							Xiaomi repacking the repositories and re-pushing the images, but it is an inference: we found
							no statement from Xiaomi. The pre-fix images are no longer tagged, so the earlier count
							cannot be reproduced from public artefacts.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 03</span><span class="ttl">// the mtime side channel</span><span class="meta"
							>85.3% of tasks</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="the mtime side channel">
						<h2>The files the fix touched are newer</h2>
						<p>
							Vals AI's report noted in passing that <code class="code-inline">find -newermt</code> can reveal
							the files the reference solution modified. The measurements below are ours: how often the
							leak is still present on the current images, how precisely it points at the fix, and what it
							is worth to an agent.
						</p>
						<p>
							In <strong>256 of 300</strong> tasks (85.3%, 95% CI 80.9–88.9%), the base checkout contains a
							separate, later cluster of tracked files (median gap 424 s), and that cluster contains at
							least one source file. In 213 of 300 (71.0%, 65.6–75.8%) the cluster also contains files
							touched by the task's test patch, or their directories.
						</p>
						<p>
							Listing files by modification time, newest first, therefore shows which files the reference
							fix changed. Where we could match a task to its upstream fix on GitHub, the first spot check
							found the late cluster equal to the fix's file set in <strong>13 of 13</strong> tasks
							(precision and recall 1.0). Extending the check anchored 75 more tasks: in 61 of them the late
							set exactly equalled the fix (mean precision 0.915, recall 0.964). Anchored tasks may be
							biased toward simple, single-commit fixes.
						</p>
						<div class="table-wrap">
							<table class="v9-table">
								<caption>
									Ranking files to find the upstream fix · 75 anchored tasks · mean with bootstrap 95%
									CI
								</caption>
								<thead>
									<tr class="head">
										<th>Ranker</th>
										<th>hit@1</th>
										<th>hit@5</th>
										<th>precision@3</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									{
										rankers.map((r) => (
											<tr>
												<td class="label">{r.ranker}</td>
												<td>{r.hit1}</td>
												<td>{r.hit5}</td>
												<td>{r.p3}</td>
											</tr>
										))
									}
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
						<p>
							The signal is strong offline: a file from the upstream fix is ranked first in 92% of these
							tasks, and appears in the top three in all of them.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 04</span><span class="ttl">// what it is worth to an agent</span><span
							class="meta">73 paired tasks</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="what it is worth to an agent">
						<h2>Paired runs, timestamps on and off</h2>
						<p>
							We ran an agent on 73 tasks twice each: once with the original timestamps (ORIG) and once with
							every modification time under the repository set to the epoch (NORM). Same task, same image,
							same prompt, condition order set by a seeded coin. The model was <code class="code-inline"
								>deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017</code
							> at temperature 0, with a 40-step cap. The primary set of 30 pairs and the analysis were
							pre-registered before any agent run; every later amendment is documented. Grading applies the task's test patch after the agent
							finishes and runs the task's test command.
						</p>
						<p>We ran three arms:</p>
						<ul>
							<li>
								<strong>Uninformed.</strong> The prompt never mentions timestamps. In 146 runs and 3,488
								commands, no agent ever sorted or filtered files by time.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Informed.</strong> One line added to the system prompt suggests <code
									class="code-inline">ls -lt</code
								> or <code class="code-inline">find . -newer</code>. Only 27 of 73 ORIG runs ran a
								time-based command before their first edit, and only 4 used a file-level <code
									class="code-inline">find</code
								>.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Scaffold.</strong> The harness itself runs a fixed ranking command and puts its
								output in the first message, so the agent always sees the late cluster under ORIG. A
								late-cluster file was ranked first in 72 of 73 ORIG runs.
							</li>
						</ul>
						<div class="table-wrap">
							<table class="v9-table">
								<caption>
									Pass counts, ORIG vs NORM · exact McNemar p · paired bootstrap 95% CI for the
									difference
								</caption>
								<thead>
									<tr class="head">
										<th>Arm</th>
										<th>Set</th>
										<th>Pairs</th>
										<th>ORIG</th>
										<th>NORM</th>
										<th>Discordant</th>
										<th>p</th>
										<th>ORIG − NORM</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									{
										arms.map((r) => (
											<tr>
												<td class="label">{r.arm}</td>
												<td>{r.set}</td>
												<td>{r.pairs}</td>
												<td>{r.orig}</td>
												<td>{r.norm}</td>
												<td>{r.disc}</td>
												<td>{r.p}</td>
												<td>{r.diff}</td>
											</tr>
										))
									}
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
						<p>
							The pre-registered decision rule for the scaffold arm uses all 73 pairs and a +15 pp
							threshold. The result, +12.3 pp, is below it. The primary 30 look stronger (p = 0.035), but
							the decision rule names all 73 and is applied as written.
						</p>
						<p>
							Most of the scaffold gap comes from the NORM control doing badly, not from ORIG doing well.
							Scaffold ORIG passed 51 of 73, against 50 for the uninformed ORIG runs. Scaffold NORM passed
							42, against 47 for the uninformed NORM runs. The NORM control saw a header that promised a
							ranking, followed by an empty list, so it is not a neutral control.
						</p>
						<p>
							Why the effect is small: the issue text already leads this agent to the files the leak
							reveals. A late-cluster file was opened before the first edit in 71 of 73 ORIG and 72 of 73
							NORM runs in the uninformed arm. File localisation, the only thing this leak gives away, is
							rarely the bottleneck for these tasks (an inference from the run data).
						</p>
						<p>
							Limits. One cheap model, one scaffold, image size capped at 1.2 GB, and no gold patch. At
							this noise level, with all 73 pairs, the effect detectable with 80% power is about +15 pp. The
							result is evidence against large effects, not evidence of no effect.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 05</span><span class="ttl">// severity</span><span class="meta">Low</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="severity">
						<h2>Low, with a stated upper bound</h2>
						<p>
							<strong>Impact: I2.</strong> The leak reveals the scope of the fix, not the solution.
							<strong>Reach: R4.</strong> Exploiting it takes ordinary tool use, such as <code
								class="code-inline">ls -t</code
							> or <code class="code-inline">find -newer</code>. Together these rate <strong>Low</strong>.
						</p>
						<p>
							We would move it to Medium if the leak materially raised solve rate. None of the arms reached
							that bar. The forced-compliance arm came closest, at +12.3 pp on all 73 pairs against a +15 pp
							threshold, but its control saw an empty list. Against a same-length list of random files (§
							07) the gap is 0.0 pp, with an upper bound of +12.3 pp. The final call is Low, and the finding
							is an efficiency gain: the first edit comes about 3 steps sooner.
						</p>
						<p>
							"Low" describes present-day use by agents that have not learned the trick. It does not
							describe the information content, which is high: an agent or policy that learns to look at
							modification times, for example through RL on these images, gets a near-perfect file
							locator. That route to Medium is untested here.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 06</span><span class="ttl">// the fix</span><span class="meta">touch</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="the fix">
						<h2>Normalise timestamps when building the image</h2>
						<p>
							Setting every modification time under the repository to one value removes the late cluster.
							This is the command we used for the NORM condition:
						</p>
						<p>
							<code class="code-inline">find &lt;repo&gt; -xdev -exec touch -h -d @0 {'{}'} +</code>
						</p>
						<p>
							It covers files, directories and symlinks, including <code class="code-inline">.git</code>.
							Afterwards, <code class="code-inline">find &lt;repo&gt; -newermt @1</code> returned 0 entries
							in 73 of 73 runs. In fresh containers with no agent changes, normalisation alone never
							changed a grade.
						</p>
						<p>
							Two side effects to know about. It makes the git index stat-dirty, and it can change build
							tools' rebuild decisions. And it only covers the repository: copies of project files elsewhere
							on disk, such as site-packages or caches, keep their times.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 07</span><span class="ttl">// sham-list control</span><span
							class="meta">73 paired tasks</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="sham-list control">
						<h2>Against a matched control, no solve-rate gain</h2>
						<p>
							The scaffold arm's NORM control saw the ranking header followed by an empty list. To test
							whether the empty list, rather than the ranking, caused the gap, we ran the NORM condition
							again with a sham list in place of the empty one: the same header, the same number of lines
							and the same timestamps as the real list, but naming randomly chosen repository files. None
							of the 73 sham lists contained a late-cluster file. Everything else matched the scaffold arm,
							and the scaffold ORIG runs were reused as the comparison. The lists, the design and the
							decision rule were fixed before any run: a gap of +15 pp or more on all 73 pairs would move
							the rating to Medium.
						</p>
						<p>
							Scaffold ORIG and the sham control each passed 51 of 73 (11 : 11 discordant, p = 1.0). The
							difference is 0.0 pp, with a 95% interval of −12.3 to +12.3 pp, below the threshold. On the
							pre-registered 30 it was 22 against 20 (+6.7 pp, −13.3 to +26.7). The sham control passed 51
							against 42 for the empty-list control (+12.3 pp, 0.0 to +24.7). So the scaffold-arm gap came
							from the empty list, not from the ranking.
						</p>
						<div class="table-wrap">
							<table class="v9-table">
								<caption>
									Scaffold ORIG vs sham-list control, all 73 pairs · means or counts · paired bootstrap
									95% CI for the difference
								</caption>
								<thead>
									<tr class="head">
										<th>Measure</th>
										<th>ORIG list</th>
										<th>Sham list</th>
										<th>ORIG − sham</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									<tr>
										<td class="label">First-edit step (no edit counted as 41)</td>
										<td>14.66</td>
										<td>17.74</td>
										<td>−3.08 (−5.01 to −1.23)</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td class="label">Steps per run (failures counted at 40)</td>
										<td>27.21</td>
										<td>30.18</td>
										<td>−2.97 (−5.73 to −0.11)</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td class="label">Steps, both passed (40 pairs)</td>
										<td>20.45</td>
										<td>25.63</td>
										<td>−5.18 (−8.23 to −2.33)</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td class="label">Files opened before first edit</td>
										<td>6.38</td>
										<td>7.26</td>
										<td>−0.88 (−1.59 to −0.23)</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td class="label">Runs stopped at the step cap</td>
										<td>11</td>
										<td>20</td>
										<td>−12.3 pp (−21.9 to −2.7)</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td class="label">Runs that searched git history</td>
										<td>13</td>
										<td>30</td>
										<td>−23.3 pp (−35.6 to −11.0)</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td class="label">Cost per run</td>
										<td>$0.0184</td>
										<td>$0.0283</td>
										<td>−$0.0098 (−$0.0173 to −$0.0028)</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td class="label">Cost per solved task</td>
										<td>$0.0264</td>
										<td>$0.0405</td>
										<td>–</td>
									</tr>
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
						<p>
							The ranking does buy speed. With the real list the agent made its first edit about 3 steps
							sooner, opened fewer files first, searched git history less often, and hit the 40-step cap 11
							times against 20. Where it edited did not change: the first edit was in a file on the real
							list in 61 against 63 runs, and no sham-control run edited a file from the sham list. The sham
							list did not mislead the agent, and the real list only got it to the right files sooner.
						</p>
						<p>
							The speed-up is clearest on the 37 tasks whose late cluster is short (1 to 10 files), includes
							source and overlaps the tests or the upstream fix: the first edit came 3.19 steps sooner (1.22
							to 5.30) and runs took 4.70 fewer steps (0.19 to 9.27), with 26 against 24 solved. On the other
							36 tasks the first edit came 2.97 steps sooner, but the interval reaches zero (−0.06 to 6.25),
							and they solved 25 against 27. By size of the late cluster, the first-edit gain was 3.33 steps
							for 1–2 files (24 tasks), 3.63 for 3–4 files (30 tasks) and 1.56 for 5–10 files (16 tasks,
							interval crossing zero). No subgroup shows a solve-rate gain. These splits are exploratory and
							small.
						</p>
						<div class="callout" role="note">
							<span class="lbl">// summary</span>
							<p>
								Xiaomi's fix for the git-history leak holds. A second leak, through file modification
								times, is present in 85% of tasks (256 of 300), and ranking files by modification time put a
								late-cluster file first in 72 of 73 tested tasks. An agent shown that ranking reaches the
								right files about 3.1 steps sooner (95% CI 1.2 to 5.0), stops at the step cap less often (11
								against 20 of 73) and costs less per solved task ($0.0264 against $0.0405), but it solves
								no more tasks: 51 against 51 of 73, with an upper bound of +12.3 pp. Severity: Low (I2,
								R4), with the efficiency gain as the finding. Normalising timestamps when building the
								images removes the signal: afterwards no file was newer than the epoch in 292 of 292 runs
								checked.
							</p>
						</div>
					</section>

					<footer class="foot">
						<div class="a">Cupel · Inductive ML</div>
						<div class="b">MiMo audit · draft · October 2026</div>
						<div class="c"><a href="/cupel">Cupel ↗</a></div>
					</footer>
				</div>
			</div>
		</main>
		<SiteFooter animate={true} />
</Layout>

